WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
Europe was still in the Dark Ages.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:07.590
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here again, and
today I wanna talk about what I call

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.910
the magic of language learning, the
emotional side of language learning.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.790
Now, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.230
Click on the bell for notifications,
and if you follow me on a podcast

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.890
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:21.830
I do appreciate it.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.265
So, you know, in my previous
video I talked about how important

00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:28.125
it was to reward ourselves.

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:31.455
For every little thing you do, every
time you are able to do something

00:00:31.455 --> 00:00:33.255
in the language, reward yourself.

00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:37.695
But there's another aspect to
rewarding yourself, and that is to

00:00:37.695 --> 00:00:42.315
indulge in those things that make
the language interesting to you.

00:00:42.735 --> 00:00:47.625
And even if you are not doing these
things in the target language, if you

00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:51.950
are cultivating that interest, if you
are cultivating the magic, you are

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.670
going to motivate yourself to learn
the language, and I wanna talk about

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.430
some examples from my own experience.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.670
So first of all, when I started
learning French, I had a professor

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:09.140
who made French culture French
civilization, very interesting to me.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.750
As a result, I started
watching French movies.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:15.590
Prior to that, I wasn't interested
in French, even though we had it

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.120
at school and so I couldn't speak.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:22.965
And then when I followed my
interests In French movies, French...

00:01:23.445 --> 00:01:26.085
everything to do with France,
and eventually ended up going

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.005
to France, that spurred me on.

00:01:28.274 --> 00:01:29.475
The same with Chinese.

00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:33.914
I got very interested in basically the
modern history of China, the twenties

00:01:33.914 --> 00:01:39.795
and thirties as China struggled, sort
of coming into contact with Western

00:01:39.795 --> 00:01:42.495
civilization, Western imperialists.

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:48.884
Uh, a very proud and, and old culture
having to face certain new realities.

00:01:48.884 --> 00:01:55.785
That whole romance of that period
motivated me to learn, uh, Chinese and

00:01:55.789 --> 00:01:57.975
I can go on and on with other examples.

00:01:57.975 --> 00:02:03.975
Russian, when I watched ... a movie,
a Russian movie from the 19th century.

00:02:04.725 --> 00:02:08.315
Uh, it conjures up this romantic
image may not be an accurate

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.945
reflection of what Russia is or was,
but it's part of what motivated me.

00:02:12.945 --> 00:02:16.394
It becomes the, the sort of
magic, the emotions, because

00:02:16.394 --> 00:02:17.565
emotions are very important.

00:02:17.894 --> 00:02:23.830
We know from cognitive science research
that when we learn something, if the

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:28.960
brain sees things that are new and
meaningful to the brain, the brain is

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.060
more likely to remember those things.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.750
And so that emotional side, that
emotional connection is important.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.250
I find that if I listen to something
in the target language and I enjoy,

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:43.210
I like the voice, I enjoy the subject
matter I'm more likely to remember,

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.250
I'm more, more likely to learn from it.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.290
Another example is my current
experience with, uh, Persian,

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.830
Turkish, Arabic, that, you know,
central Asia, middle Eastern world.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.660
So in addition to what I do on LingQ
where I study and I explore some

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.470
of the great content that we have,
for example, in Turkish and in, in

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.730
Persian, that Sahra has prepared for
me on Iranian history and so forth.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.880
I also indulge my interest
by reading in English.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.430
Uh, so I wanted to show you some examples.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:20.475
So here's a book for example about Selim,
who was one of the great emperors of

00:03:20.475 --> 00:03:26.685
the Ottoman Empire and how he, you know,
killed his brothers and fought to gain the

00:03:26.685 --> 00:03:27.870
throne and what he did with the throne.

00:03:28.215 --> 00:03:32.685
I just started into the book and it
is fascinating, uh, and it conjures

00:03:32.685 --> 00:03:38.605
up that Turkish world, the Turkey
connecting with the Safavid, the Shiite,

00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:42.255
uh, forces in, uh, next door, Iran.

00:03:42.885 --> 00:03:46.785
All of that is part of
that Turkish/Iranian world.

00:03:47.415 --> 00:03:50.205
And as I get into that, of
course I get into the whole

00:03:50.685 --> 00:03:53.565
magic again of Central Asia.

00:03:53.985 --> 00:03:59.115
Central Asia a thousand years ago
was a major center of world learning.

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:04.845
We sometimes forget that, uh, at a time
when, uh, Europe was still in the Dark

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:10.035
Ages, there were scientists in Central
Asia who were measuring the stars

00:04:10.035 --> 00:04:15.660
and, you know, the orbits of, of , you
know, planets and this kind of thing.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.959
Uh, and their language was Persian.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.000
In fact, here's another book.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.320
I, I, I tend to go buy books because
I, I want to indulge my interest

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.915
because that then motivates me to
work harder on my Persian, my Turkish,

00:04:31.065 --> 00:04:34.185
my Arabic, um, The Persianate World.

00:04:34.485 --> 00:04:39.195
Um, one that I started into, by the
way, is The Lost Enlightenment, which

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:47.265
again, deals with very specifically
this Central Asia Bohara, uh, Samarkand

00:04:47.385 --> 00:04:52.425
center of learning for quite a few
hundred years, which contributed to

00:04:52.515 --> 00:04:54.675
the modern world as we now know it.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.549
All of that is a bit of a digression
and a distraction, but I don't

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:03.539
begrudge myself the time that I spend
reading in English about all of these

00:05:03.539 --> 00:05:09.390
things because it motivates me in
my learning of these languages and

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.120
my discovery, uh, of these cultures.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:18.105
Because inevitably when we learn
the language, we discover culture.

00:05:18.105 --> 00:05:22.185
We discover new areas that previously
were perhaps a collection of

00:05:22.185 --> 00:05:24.615
stereotypes come alive for us.

00:05:24.885 --> 00:05:28.875
So I suggest you do indulge your
interests, whatever they may be.

00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:32.985
It might be Anime in the case of
Japanese, or K-Pop in the case of

00:05:32.985 --> 00:05:35.295
Korean, whatever those interests are.

00:05:35.655 --> 00:05:40.155
Or if you wanna watch a movie with, you
know, subtitles in your own language,

00:05:40.185 --> 00:05:41.955
even if you don't understand much.

00:05:42.315 --> 00:05:44.625
This was the case when
I started into Russian.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:49.229
It's, um, it's, it may not do that much
for your language learning, but it is

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:53.849
stimulating, it triggers that interest and
that's so important in language learning.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.460
And I did do, uh, a couple of
videos one 13 years ago, one nine

00:05:59.465 --> 00:06:04.140
years ago on the importance of
our emotions in language learning.

00:06:04.919 --> 00:06:07.349
So I suggest you have a
look at those as well.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:08.640
Thank you for listening.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:09.419
Bye for now.

